---
import ToolPageLayout from '../layouts/ToolPageLayout.astro';
import OvertimeCalculator from '../islands/OvertimeCalculator';
import laborRules from '../data/labor/2026.json';

const meta = laborRules.meta;
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How is overtime pay computed in the Philippines?',
    a: 'Overtime — work beyond 8 hours a day — is paid at your hourly rate plus at least 25% on an ordinary day (125%). On a rest day, special day or holiday, overtime is the day’s premium rate plus 30%. Example: ₱100/hour on an ordinary day → ₱125 per OT hour.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the overtime rates per day type?',
    a: 'Ordinary day: 125%. Rest day or special day: 169% (130% × 130%). Special day falling on a rest day: 195%. Regular holiday: 260% (200% × 130%). Regular holiday falling on a rest day: 338%. All rates are per hour beyond 8 hours, as percentages of the ordinary hourly rate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I get my hourly rate from my monthly salary?',
    a: 'Divide your daily rate by 8. If you only know your monthly salary, the usual conversion is (monthly salary × 12) ÷ number of paid working days per year ÷ 8 — commonly 313 days for Mon–Sat schedules or 261 for Mon–Fri. Your HR’s divisor governs; the calculator’s monthly option uses 313 as an approximation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is overtime on a night shift paid more?',
    a: 'Yes. Overtime hours that fall between 10 PM and 6 AM also earn the 10% night differential on top of the OT rate. An ordinary-day night OT hour pays 125% × 110% = 137.5% of the ordinary hourly rate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can my employer refuse to pay overtime?',
    a: 'Statutory overtime pay is mandatory for covered employees who are required or permitted to work beyond 8 hours. However, managerial employees, field personnel and certain other categories are excluded from hours-of-work rules. Undertime on another day cannot be offset against overtime.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is overtime computed for monthly-paid employees?',
    a: 'Convert the monthly salary to an hourly rate first — monthly × 12 ÷ factor ÷ 8, using 365 if you are paid for every day of the year, 313 for a six-day week or 261 for a five-day week — then apply the overtime rate for the day type: 125% on an ordinary day, 169% on a rest day or special day, 260% on a regular holiday. Example: ₱20,000 monthly on a 261 factor is ₱114.94 an hour, so one ordinary-day OT hour pays ₱143.68.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can undertime be offset by overtime?',
    a: 'No. Article 88 of the Labor Code prohibits offsetting: undertime on one day cannot be deducted from overtime worked on another day, and overtime must still be paid at the premium rate even if you left early on a different day. Undertime may be deducted from pay at the ordinary hourly rate, but never netted against OT hours.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much is overtime pay per hour in the Philippines in 2026?',
    a: 'There is no fixed peso amount — the rate is a percentage of your own hourly wage: 125% on an ordinary working day, 169% on a rest day or special non-working day, 195% on a special day that is also your rest day, 260% on a regular holiday and 338% on a regular holiday falling on your rest day. At ₱100 an hour, one OT hour pays ₱125, ₱169, ₱195, ₱260 or ₱338 respectively.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Magkano ang overtime pay per hour?',
    a: 'On an ordinary working day, overtime is paid at 125% of your hourly rate: at ₱100 per hour each OT hour is ₱125. The rate rises to 169% on a rest day or special day, 195% on a special day that is also your rest day, 260% on a regular holiday and 338% on a regular holiday that falls on your rest day.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Paano kinukwenta ang overtime sa rest day?',
    a: 'Work on a rest day is paid 130% for the first 8 hours; overtime beyond 8 hours is that rate plus 30%, or 130% × 130% = 169% of the ordinary hourly rate. Example: ₱100 per hour × 2 OT hours × 169% = ₱338.',
  },
];
---

<ToolPageLayout
  title="Overtime Pay Calculator Philippines 2026 – OT Rates per Hour"
  description="Compute Philippine overtime pay: 125% on ordinary days, 169% on rest days, 260% on regular holidays — with night differential handling and full breakdown."
  path="/overtime-pay-calculator/"
  h1="Overtime Pay Calculator Philippines"
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['nightDiff', 'holidayPay', 'takeHome', 'finalPay']}
>
  <Fragment slot="answer">
    <p>
      Overtime pay in the Philippines is your hourly rate <strong>plus at least 25% for work
      beyond 8 hours on an ordinary day (125%)</strong>, and the day's premium rate
      <strong>plus 30%</strong> on rest days, special days and holidays — e.g., 169% on a rest
      day and 260% on a regular holiday. Enter your rate, day type and OT hours below for the
      exact breakdown.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="calculator">
    <OvertimeCalculator client:idle />
  </Fragment>

  <h2>Overtime Rates by Day Type (per OT hour)</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Day Type</th><th>First 8 Hours</th><th>OT Rate (beyond 8h)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Ordinary day</td><td>100%</td><td>125%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rest day</td><td>130%</td><td>169%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Special (non-working) day</td><td>130%</td><td>169%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Special day + rest day</td><td>150%</td><td>195%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Regular holiday</td><td>200%</td><td>260%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Regular holiday + rest day</td><td>260%</td><td>338%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-ink-soft">
    Percentages are of the ordinary hourly rate. Holiday scenarios are an input dimension here; for
    the full day's pay including the first 8 hours, use the
    <a href="/holiday-pay-calculator/">Holiday Pay Calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How It Is Calculated</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Base hourly rate</strong> — daily rate ÷ 8, or from your monthly salary via your company's divisor.</li>
    <li><strong>Day-type multiplier</strong> — 100% ordinary, 130% rest/special day, 200% regular holiday, etc.</li>
    <li><strong>OT factor</strong> — +25% on ordinary days, +30% on all premium days, applied on the day-type rate.</li>
    <li><strong>Night differential</strong> — OT hours between 10 PM and 6 AM add 10% on the OT rate.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example Computations</h2>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example 1 — 2 OT hours on an ordinary day, ₱100/hour:</strong> ₱100 × 125% × 2 =
    <strong>₱250</strong> additional pay.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example 2 — 3 OT hours on a rest day, ₱80/hour:</strong> ₱80 × 169% × 3 =
    <strong>₱405.60</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example 3 — 2 OT hours on a regular holiday, both at night, ₱100/hour:</strong> OT
    rate ₱260/hour; night differential adds ₱26/hour → (₱260 + ₱26) × 2 = <strong>₱572</strong>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Overtime Pay Computation From a Monthly Salary</h2>
  <p>
    Overtime is always paid per hour, so a monthly salary must first become an hourly rate:
    <strong>hourly rate = monthly salary × 12 ÷ factor ÷ 8</strong>. The factor is the number of
    paid days in your year — 365 if you are paid for every day including rest days and holidays
    (most monthly-paid employees), 313 for a six-day week not paid on rest days, or 261 for a
    five-day week. Then apply the day-type rate from the table above. Your HR’s factor governs;
    the calculator’s monthly option uses 313.
  </p>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example — ₱20,000 monthly, 2 OT hours on an ordinary day:</strong> with factor 261,
    daily rate ₱919.54 and hourly ₱114.94 → OT ₱114.94 × 125% × 2 = <strong>₱287.36</strong>.
    With factor 313: hourly ₱95.85 → <strong>₱239.62</strong>. With factor 365: hourly ₱82.19 →
    <strong>₱205.48</strong>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who This Applies To</h2>
  <p>
    Covered rank-and-file employees under the Labor Code's hours-of-work rules. Managerial
    employees, field personnel, family members dependent on the employer for support, domestic
    helpers and workers paid by results are generally excluded. Compressed workweek arrangements
    approved by DOLE may treat hours differently — check your company's registered scheme.
  </p>

  <h2>Official Source</h2>
  <p>
    Labor Code of the Philippines, Arts. 87–90, as summarized in the
    <a href={meta.official_source_url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOLE Handbook on Workers' Statutory Monetary Benefits</a>.
    Last verified {new Date(meta.last_verified + 'T00:00:00').toLocaleDateString('en-PH', { month: 'long', day: 'numeric', year: 'numeric' })}.
  </p>
</ToolPageLayout>
